<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<assembly manifestVersion="1.0" xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1">
  <assemblyIdentity version="1.0.0.0" name="K617Mod.Ui" />

  <!--
    requireAdministrator, not asInvoker.

    Suppression opens the Interception driver's device files
    (\\.\interception00 .. 19). Those need elevation. Without this
    manifest, double-clicking the exe launches it unelevated, the
    pipeline starts, suppression fails, and the app lands in its
    "Running, but suppression FAILED - keys also type" state: the
    controller works but the keyboard types into the game at the same
    time. That is a confusing failure to hand someone who just wanted to
    run the program, and it is the one thing standing between "it's an
    exe" and "it works when you double-click it".

    The cost is a UAC prompt on every launch, and that a
    requireAdministrator app cannot be started from the ordinary
    HKCU\...\Run registry key - a "start with Windows" feature would
    need a Task Scheduler task with highest privileges instead. Worth
    knowing before that gets built.
  -->
  <trustInfo xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v2">
    <security>
      <requestedPrivileges xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
        <requestedExecutionLevel level="requireAdministrator" uiAccess="false" />
      </requestedPrivileges>
    </security>
  </trustInfo>

  <compatibility xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:compatibility.v1">
    <application>
      <!-- Windows 10 and 11. Declaring these stops Windows applying
           compatibility shims meant for older programs. -->
      <supportedOS Id="{8e0f7a12-bfb3-4fe8-b9a5-48fd50a15a9a}" />
      <supportedOS Id="{1f676c76-80e1-4239-95bb-83d0f6d0da78}" />
      <supportedOS Id="{4a2f28e3-53b9-4441-ba9c-d69d4a4a6e38}" />
    </application>
  </compatibility>

  <!--
    DPI awareness is deliberately NOT declared here, even though the
    curve editor draws to exact pixel coordinates and wants
    PerMonitorV2 to avoid being bitmap-stretched on a scaled display.

    UseWindowsForms=true (needed for the tray icon) pulls in WinForms'
    own manifest-generation machinery, which also wants to own the DPI
    settings. Declaring them here too gave dotnet publish two manifest
    fragments competing for one output slot - MSB3094 ("SourceFiles
    refers to 2 item(s)... DestinationFiles refers to 1"), confirmed by
    the WFAC010 warning printed right before it: "Remove high DPI
    settings from app.manifest and configure via ... 'ApplicationHighDpiMode'
    project property". PerMonitorV2 is set the WinForms-supported way
    instead, via ApplicationHighDpiMode in K617Mod.Ui.csproj - it is a
    process-wide Win32 setting, so it covers the WPF windows too, not
    just WinForms' own tray icon.
  -->
</assembly>
